 17592186423850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423859 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140963" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423859 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423859 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the $3$rd column of the matrix $U?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423859 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7cbede22-0844-5470-8655-1901a08d01cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423859 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{6}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=1.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the $1$st and $2$nd columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the third column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0,\\end{aligned}\\,‖{u}_{3}‖=1.\n$$\n\nSolving this system, which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{30}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}}{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the nonzero normalized solution \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $U:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{30}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423860 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423860 #edb/ref 1069 "q-98641" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423860 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423860 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\,\\,2 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singula